 **Gas Town v1.0.0 by Steve Yegge**:\n\n## Key Information\n\n**Release Announcement:**\n- Steve Yegge released Gas Town v1.0.0 in **April 2026**\n- He wrote a blog post titled \"Gas Town: from Clown Show to v1.0\" announcing this milestone release\n- The release followed a \"wild 3-month ride\" of development\n- This was version 1.0.0 after 14 releases (v0.5.0 through v0.13.0) of iterative development\n\n## About Gas Town\n\n**What it is:**\n- A **multi-agent workspace manager** for coordinating multiple AI coding agents\n- Supports agents like Claude Code, GitHub Copilot, Codex, Gemini, and others\n- Designed to orchestrate dozens of AI coding agents working simultaneously\n- Includes a companion system called \"Beads\" (a shared memory system)\n\n**Key Features:**\n- Persistent work tracking\n- Multi-agent orchestration system\n- Allows developers to run 20-30 (or more) AI agents in parallel\n- Manages complex coding tasks across multiple AI instances\n\n**Context:**\n- Steve Yegge is a former Amazon/Google senior engineer and legendary tech blogger\n- He reportedly ran 320 Claude Code instances on his machine\n- The project represents his vision of humans as \"shepherds\" of AI agent swarms rather than direct code producers\n- Open source project available on GitHub under the gastownhall organization\n\n**Official Resources:**\n- GitHub: https://github.com/gastownhall/gastown\n- Documentation: https://gastown.dev/\n- Blog announcement: https://steve-yegge.medium.com/gas-town-from-clown-show-to-v1-0\n\nThe v1.0.0 release marked production stability for this innovative multi-agent AI coding orchestration system.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."
